title: ocp-cache pulls large images at ~1 MB/s; Docker Hub direct gives 200 MB/s
updated: 2026-10-16T07:30:43Z
---

Observed on cluster citiai, 2026-10-03: four nodes pulled vllm/vllm-openai:v0.27.1 (~7 GiB compressed) through the docker.io mirror ocp-cache (registries.yaml: docker.io -> http://localhost:30500, NodePort of ocp-system/ocp-cache). After 45 min the ingest on crag stood at 3.7 GiB; the cache pod showed ~660m CPU and 59 MiB RAM. The same image pulled with ctr directly from registry-1.docker.io on crag and otho-lab ran at 200+ MiB/s and finished in minutes.

So the pull-through cache is the bottleneck for big images, and with several nodes pulling the same image at once it gets worse (each node waits on the same slow upstream fetch).

To look at:
- what limits it: single replica, storage backend (emptyDir/hostPath on cp-lab?), proxy chunking, CPU limit
- whether containerd falls back to the upstream when the mirror is slow (it does not — a mirror that answers is used)
- options: more resources, a faster backend, or let big images bypass the mirror (e.g. per-registry override in registries.yaml)

Workaround used: ctr -n k8s.io images pull --platform linux/amd64 docker.io/... on each node, and for arm64 an import from the local Docker (docker save | ctr images import).

Live 2026-10-16 Runde 6 (frank-ocp, ocp-lab:6801f92) gemessen (Details im Commit ec87339 und .agent-sessions/ocp-lab/ergebnis-turn6.md): Cache ist nicht der Engpass, kalte Pulls laufen im Tempo des Docker-Hub-CDN (2-27 MiB/s je Verbindung, mit und ohne Cache), warmer Cache 44 MiB/s. Gebaut: registry-Image gepinnt (2.8.3). Ideen ohne Messbeleg, nicht gebaut: Vorwärmen großer Images, mehrere Cache-Pods (paralleles Ziehen mehrerer Nodes nicht gemessen).

